  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nPleasant seasonable weather as we start August this weekend with\nafternoon highs generally in the low 80s today 7 tomorrow. Areas\nof mist & fog are likely to develop again overnight into\ntomorrow morning, with the most widespread fog likely in low-\nlying areas of western Wisconsin & southeast Minnesota. The\nupper level pattern becomes more active early next week as a \nshortwave trough deepens as it moves eastwards along the US-\nCanada border. Most of the widespread rain & thunderstorms of \nthis system will be tied to the stronger upper-level forcing \nacross northern Minnesota, but we will likely see scattered \nshowers & thunderstorms develop along the surface cold front \nextending southwards through the area. Most model guidance \nbrings the timing of the front through the area during the \novernight hours Monday into tuesday, not the most favorable time\nof day for instability & thunderstorm development. Thus we \nshould see some sort of rain across at least western Minnesota \nlate Monday night into Tuesday, but any widespread \nthunderstorms or a good soaking rain will likely be waning by \nthe time the front reaches our area.\n\nWe return back to a seasonable early-Agugust pattern for \nmidweek with weak high pressure over the upper midwest. A slow-\nmoving front south of this region of high pressure will light up\nwith thunderstorms & potentially heavy rain later in the week, \nbut the general model consensus keeps this activity south of the\narea across Iowa & the mid-Missippi valley. It's still a ways \nout but there appears to be a better shot at more widespread \nrain across MN/WI late next week into next weekend as most \nguidance depicts another shortwave passing through the Upper \nMidwest.",
